Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering
Professor / Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in Intelligent Railway Engineering under Strategic Hiring Scheme
(Ref. 261021009)
The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) and the Hong Kong Branch of National Rail Transit Electrification and Automation Engineering Technology Research Center (CNERC-Rail) invite applications from highly motivated and suitably qualified candidates with research expertise in intelligent railway technologies, including smart materials and structures, railway monitoring and control, and intelligent maintenance of rail infrastructure.
The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering has a strong international reputation for conducting world-class research that delivers societal benefits and maintains well-established partnerships with industry and government agencies in Hong Kong and beyond. It is ranked first worldwide in Transportation Science and Technology in the Shanghai-Ranking Global Ranking of Academic Subjects 2025, the 2nd in Civil Engineering in the U.S. News Best Global Universities Rankings 2026, the 18th in Civil and Structural Engineering, and 34th in Environmental Science in the QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026.
The Department offers academic programmes at the various levels (PhD, MSc, BEng, BSc). It currently comprises approximately 80 faculty members (more than 40 regular faculty members at Assistant Professor or above levels) and around 40 technical and administrative staff. The Department is committed to internationally recognised standards of excellence in teaching and research. For further information about CEE, please visit https://www.polyu.edu.hk/cee/ .
CNERC-Rail is a national-level, mission-driven innovation hub dedicated to advancing safer, smarter and lower-carbon rail transit systems. Building on The Hong Kong Polytechnic University’s established strengths and global reputation in rail transit, intelligent infrastructure and smart cities, the Center collaborates with industry partners, government agencies and leading universities in the Chinese Mainland and worldwide to conduct pioneering research and translate research outcomes into practical applications. The Center aspires to become a world-leading research and development hub for intelligent operation and maintenance technologies and a major platform for developing talent in related fields. For further information about the Center, please visit https://www.polyu.edu.hk/cnerc-rail/ .
The Hong Kong Special Administrative Region is an international city located strategically at the heart of Asia and is the gateway to the Chinese Mainland, with excellent transport links to major international cities.
To boost the leadership and impact of PolyU’s research work in areas of significant societal needs and strategic importance, the Strategic Hiring Scheme is introduced to recruit faculty members at all levels and outstanding scholars are invited to join the University under the Strategic Hiring Scheme. Appointees recruited under the Strategic Hiring Scheme should have conducted / led world-leading research that is commensurate with the stage of their academic career.
Duties
The appointee will be affiliated with the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering and serve as a key member of CNERC-Rail, with the potential to assume a future leadership role as Director or Associate Director of the Center. The appointee will lead the development and delivery of new postgraduate programmes in Railway Engineering or Transportation Engineering. The appointee will be required to:
(a) teach undergraduate and postgraduate subjects in Railway Engineering and related fields, such as Transit Engineering, Intelligent Infrastructure, Transportation Engineering, Sensing and Monitoring, Energy Systems, and Artificial Intelligence for Infrastructure;
(b) conduct high-impact research that leads to publications in top-tier refereed journals, and actively pursue and secure competitive research funding;
(c) initiate, lead and participate in mission-driven research programmes aligned with CNERC-Rail’s objectives, including the development and commercialisation of prototypes, pilot projects and digital-twin platforms;
(d) supervise student projects and MSc and PhD dissertations;
(e) participate in scholarly, industrial and consultancy activities, including collaborations with rail transit equipment manufacturers, operators, government agencies and international partners;
(f) contribute, where appropriate, to innovation and knowledge-transfer activities;
(g) undertake academic and administrative duties at the Center, Department and University levels;
(h) develop and manage subjects, curricula and pedagogical approaches for existing and new postgraduate programmes; and
(i) perform any other duties assigned by the Director of CNERC-Rail, Head of Unit or their delegates.
Qualifications
Applicants should possess:
(a) a PhD in Railway, Civil, Transportation, Mechanical, Electrical or Electronic Engineering, Materials Science, Computer or Data Science, or a closely related discipline, which is relevant to Rail Transit Electrification, Automation, Sensing, Intelligent Operation and Maintenance, or related areas;
(b) expertise in, but not limited to, one or more of the following areas:
• high-sensitivity flexible sensors for rail transit applications (e.g. transient pressure, structural stress, wind loads, tunnel–bridge dynamic loads), including sensor networks and digital twins for full life-cycle health monitoring and early warning;
• energy harvesting devices and acoustic–electric conversion metamaterials, specifically for harvesting railway noise and vibration energy in trains, tracks, and tunnels;
• surface metamaterials / metastructures for drag reduction and aeroacoustic noise mitigation, including CFD-based design and machine-learning-driven optimisation of microstructured and bio-inspired surfaces;
• carbon–cement batteries and supercapacitors, including cement-based structural capacitors, multifunctional energy-storage/binding materials, and data-driven materials discovery using machine learning and large language models; and
• intelligent operation and maintenance systems for rail infrastructure and networks, including digital twins, multimodal sensing, AI-powered diagnostics and prognostics, predictive maintenance, and full life-cycle asset management for track and electrification systems.
(c) a strong record of high-quality, high-impact research in one or more of the areas listed above, demonstrated through achievements such as journal publications, competitive research grants, patents, prototypes or industrial collaborations;
(d) a strong commitment to excellence in teaching, research and knowledge application;
(e) demonstrated ability, or strong potential, to establish an independent, externally funded research programme and to lead or contribute to interdisciplinary, industry-engaged research projects;
(f) strong professional networks and the potential to become a world-leading scholar and academic leader within CNERC-Rail; and
(g) excellent communication skills and a high level of proficiency in English for teaching and presentations.
Applicants for appointment as Professor should demonstrate excellence in teaching, an outstanding international research reputation, and proven experience in leading academic teams. Applicants with less experience may be considered for appointment as Associate Professor or Assistant Professor. Duties and performance expectations will be commensurate with the level of appointment.
Conditions of Service
A highly competitive remuneration package will be offered. Faculty members recruited through the Strategic Hiring Scheme at Professor level will receive a start-up grant of HKD 5 million or more to support research activities (including the admission of two PhD students, purchase of equipment, hiring of research staff, etc.) in the initial five years, whereas those appointed at Associate Professor or Assistant Professor level will receive a start-up grant of HKD 3 million or more. Initial appointment for Assistant Professor will be on a fixed-term gratuity-bearing contract. Re-engagement thereafter is subject to mutual agreement. An appropriate term will be provided for appointment at Associate Professor / Professor levels. For general information on terms and conditions for appointment of academic staff in the University, please visit the website at https://www.polyu.edu.hk/hro/docdrive/careers/doc/Prof.pdf .
C onsideration of applications will commence on 20 November 2026 until the position is filled .
Apply Now
Posting date: 21 October 2026

SCHOOL OF CREATIVE ARTS
ACADEMY OF MUSIC
Impact Measurement Lead (26270274)
The School of Creative Arts (“SCA”) is managing Hong Kong Jockey Club Charities Trust Initiated Projects (“JC Projects”) which aim to bring transformative change to (i) underserved children by enhancing their social, emotional, and cognitive development through sports and music education and (ii) secondary school students by enhancing their creativity, communication, collaboration and critical thinking (4Cs) through arts and humanities themed visits, weekend and summer programs. SCA is inviting applications with research background for the post to focus on impact measurement of these transformative projects:
Responsibilities:
Lead a team to conduct impact measurement research on the JC Projects and related programs;
Contribute to the evidence-based outcome measures framework, assessment methodologies, approaches to data tracking, collecting, reporting, analysing;
Strengthen and refine key outcome indicators in a timely manner;
Stay updated on relevant social and educational developments, including government policies and similar-scale projects, to assess their impact on the project and conduct timely reviews for participant satisfaction, improved student learning experiences, and maximum project impact; and
Perform any other delegated duties as required.
Requirements:
A recognised master's degree or above, with 7 years and above experiences in educational research, action research, research methodologies, qualitative and quantitative research methods;
Experience leading a small team to carry out impact measurement;
Experience in publishing academic papers;
Experience in measurement tools and statistics, especially in relation to measuring the impact of education programs that focus on music, arts, humanities, and creativity in general;
Strong analytic and presentation skills (verbal and visual);
Excellent interpersonal, communication, and supervision skills; ability to effectively communicate with diverse stakeholders from music, social, cultural sectors, and academia; and
Result-oriented approach to impact measurement for the entire project, while remaining open-minded to different stakeholder perspectives to enhance project review and improvement.
Shortlisted candidates will be invited for a written test.
The initial appointment will be offered on a fixed-term contract of two years. Re-appointment thereafter is subject to mutual agreement and availability of funding.
Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience.
Application Procedure:
Applicants are invited to submit their applications at the HKBU e-Recruitment System. Those who are not invited for interview 8 weeks after submission of application may consider their applications unsuccessful. Details of the University's Personal Information Collection Statement can be found at https://hro.hkbu.edu.hk/en/worklife-at-hkbu/employee-favourable-environment.html#privacy-policy.
The University reserves the right not to make an appointment for the post advertised, and the appointment will be made according to the terms and conditions applicable at the time of offer.
Review of applications is ongoing until the position is filled.

Founded in 1989, Hong Kong Metropolitan University (HKMU) is a modern, vibrant and dynamic university. We tailor our professional programmes to adapt to market trends and meet industry needs, thus providing our students with quality professional education and clear career paths. Being the first University of Applied Sciences (UAS) in Hong Kong, we pledge to play a pioneering role in enhancing recognition of vocational and professional education and training, and nurturing talents with both applied skills and knowledge.
As a faculty-driven, student-centred university in support of innovative teaching and learning, strategic research, and stakeholder outreach to provide maximum benefit to our communities, we conduct research that advances knowledge and enhances teaching, focusing on strategic areas, including digital humanities and literature, international business, gerontechnology, personalised care, smart city, open and innovative education, and bilingual learning and teaching.
HKMU is becoming an ever more vital link in addressing and helping Hong Kong to solve many difficult challenges – as part of our involvement in, and commitment to, the ‘metropolis’ of Hong Kong. Our plans to expand into the Greater Bay Area (GBA) will also cultivate talent to serve Hong Kong and the wider metropolitan GBA.
For more information about the University, please visit https://www.hkmu.edu.hk .
We are now looking for a suitable person to fill the following position in the School of Open Learning:
Major Duties and Responsibilities
The appointee will be responsible mainly for the following:
Assisting in the organization and promotion of scholarly activities such as seminars and workshops;
Conducting literature review on topics related to open and innovative education;
Developing materials and tools on educational technologies;
Designing instruments for data collection;
Supporting the project team to conduct questionnaire surveys and interviews;
Carrying out qualitative and quantitative data analysis;
Assisting in the preparation of research publications and reports;
Assisting in the promotion of research in open and innovative education;
Providing assistance in a broad range of administrative duties, including preparation of project notes, meeting minutes and reports; and
Performing other duties as assigned.
Candidates
Candidates should possess the following qualifications, experience and competence:
A recognized degree (for Research Assistant II) or a Master’s degree (for Research Assistant I) in Education, Social Sciences or related areas (preference will be given to those who are familiar with research on higher education or educational technology) ;
Very good command of English and Chinese ;
Strong sense of responsibility, effective organization and interpersonal skills ; and
Able to work under pressure and meet tight deadlines .
Candidates may be invited to attend a written test.
Terms and Conditions for Appointment
Successful candidate will be appointed on a temporary part-time contract. Benefits will be provided in accordance with the statutory provisions.
To Apply
