Description
The Department of Physics at The Chinese University of Hong Kong invites applications for a Research Assistant Professor position in terahertz detector and astronomical instrumentation design .
The appointee will join the RGC Collaborative Research Fund project “Hong Kong’s Window on the Universe – First Light” , a Hong Kong-led programme developing Roger , a submillimeter/terahertz astronomical camera and polarimeter. Roger combines microwave kinetic inductance detector arrays with a cryogenic system, specialised terahertz optics, readout electronics, and novel polarimeter modules.
Major Duties
The appointee will take a leading role in the design, testing, optimisation, and integration of the project’s terahertz detector system. Major responsibilities include:
designing and optimising terahertz/submillimeter detector components and associated optical systems;
characterising microwave kinetic inductance detector arrays under cryogenic operating conditions;
contributing to the calibration and optimisation of detector arrays for astronomical observations;
completing the design and fabrication of relay optics for integrating Roger with the James Clerk Maxwell Telescope or the Greenland Telescope; and
participating in telescope installation, commissioning, testing, and first-light observations.
Qualifications
Applicants should have:
a doctoral degree in physics, astronomy, electrical or electronic engineering, applied physics, optical engineering, or a closely related discipline;
relevant postdoctoral or equivalent research experience;
demonstrated research experience in one or more of the following areas:
terahertz, millimeter-wave, or submillimeter detector technology;
cryogenic instrumentation and low-temperature measurements;
terahertz or quasi-optical system design;
detector characterisation, microwave measurements, or noise analysis;
the ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary instrumentation team.
